# Meditation

Meditation is an ancient practice of calming the mind.

The mind is a noisy place, a never-ending stream of thoughts, one overlapping another. The practice of meditation turns the practitioner inward, drawing attention toward attention itself.

To begin practicing meditation, you must first understand the sacred breath.

Breath cleanses and nourishes our bodies physically, but it is also symbolic of the gift of life itself. We see it in the changing of the seasons and the lapping of ocean waves upon a quiet shore. All creation breathes and pulses, seeking yet never finding stability.

Therefore, breathe with intention.

Notice the relationship between your breath and your thoughts, how the emotional charge of a thought might make you hold your breath or breathe a sigh of relief. Breath is intrinsically related to thought. Through the manipulation of breath, we can shift our internal state.

Slowly now, take a deep breath.

Taste the air. Notice its texture and sound. Feel it moving over your lips and across your skin. Feel the cool air washing down your throat like a holy mountain brook, nourishing the alveoli in your lungs.

Deep, deep, deep.

Slow and rhythmic.

Let the breath reach deep into your Buddha belly. Feel the belly fill from top to bottom, then slowly collapse from bottom to top.

At the bottom of the inhalation, while you hold the breath for a few proportional moments, begin flexing your pelvic floor, more specifically the modon.

Then release the waste gases and emotional charge carried by the ruminations of the day.

Another deep breath.

Notice how the expansion of your lungs and the flexion of your diaphragm massage your vital organs and nerves, gently releasing areas of stagnation and bringing healthy life force into places that were depleted.

Let yourself settle into a rhythm that feels comforting and nourishing for your body in this moment.

Now you have a basic toolkit.

Take another deep breath.

The point of hearing this is to absorb it and forget you know it, like a divine fool. This is practice.

Thoughts are probably arising: am I doing this right, am I the second coming of Christ, what’s for dinner?

Observe the thought drifting upon the stream of air carried by your breath. It approaches as you breathe in and passes as you exhale. Perhaps it goes over a waterfall or burns up in the distance.

The thought does not disappear. It goes to sleep because, right now, it is not important.

This is your time to rejuvenate and heal your nervous system and sharpen your mind.

Haunting memories, ruminations, and neurotic obsessions carry an emotional charge. They continually rise without finding a place to discharge, adding load to the nervous system and obscuring discernment.

The mind can become like a rampaging elephant, jumping compulsively from one thought to another and wreaking havoc upon the interior world.

Take a deep breath.

If your attention has drifted, slowly drop back in.

This is a workout.

A mantra gives your attention something intentional to latch onto.

As you inhale, repeat internally:

*Sat.*

As you exhale:

*Nam.*

*Sat Nam.*

True name.

Let the mantra travel upon the breath.

You may also use a short prayer.

As you inhale:

*Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God.*

As you exhale:

*Have mercy upon me, a sinner.*

You might choose another sacred phrase or become creative with what you give your attention to hold.

Now let us draw that attention into the body.

Feel the sacred breath passing your lips, sipped through your nostrils, moving through your throat and into your lungs.

Feel your feet upon the ground.

Gather your attention into the sensations there. The pressure beneath them, the movement of blood, the texture of skin against the floor. Visualize their structure beneath the surface, the bones, blood vessels, muscles, skin, and hair.

From the feet, awareness can slowly travel upward through the legs, pelvis, Buddha belly, chest, hands, arms, throat, face, and scalp.

Each region enters awareness in its own time.

Areas of discomfort can be approached with curiosity. Sensation appears, thought follows, and both are carried upon the breath.

Allow the whole body to gather into awareness as one living form.

Now feel the vast depths of the cosmos behind your head, stretching back through space and time, filled with vast burning stars held by the soft black infinite materium.

Feel your feet upon the ground.

Move your awareness downward into the soil, through roots and stone, past the hidden waters beneath the land, descending through the deep layers of the Earth toward its burning core.

Sense the weight of the world holding you.

Then widen into the life surrounding you, the plants and animals, the geographic features, the rhythm of the land itself. Sense yourself held among them in communion.

When thoughts arise, use the mantra:

*I am one with the Fullness, and the Fullness is one with me.*

Rest there.

I suggest meditating for at least twenty minutes to give yourself a chance to drop in. The first part may be noisy. Stay long enough for the deeper rhythm to become perceptible.

It is not necessary to do any odd poses. You can literally do this anywhere.

The techniques described here do not need to be practiced all at once or in a particular order. Breath, mantra, prayer, body awareness, spatial attention, and silence can be explored separately or woven together according to what feels nourishing in the moment.

This is only a lay of the land. There is much more to learn.

Once this inner movement becomes familiar, it can follow you into ordinary life.

You can practice it while talking to people through a technique called passive listening.

Drop into a meditative state and put your mind in neutral. Hone all of your attention upon listening. When the argument forming inside your head begins pulling at you, let the other person’s words return to the foreground.

The frame is empathy and the desire to understand.

Shut up your outer voice, quiet the inner one, and listen.

This receptive state allows mirroring and rapport to arise naturally. Your regulated nervous system gives the other person something stable to meet so that you can hear one another.

You can practice while walking quietly or while engaged in a dangerous or physically challenging task. Breath steadies the nervous system while attention settles into the reality in front of you.

The Navy SEALs use a technique called box breathing to regulate their nervous systems before combat. Other teachers work with different breathing rhythms, positions of the eyes, prayers, mantras, and objects of attention.

Each practice opens another direction for exploration.

Meditation is a powerful way to hone control of your attention and open space for insight and self-control. Its clinical benefits are extensive. It costs nothing and can be practiced almost anywhere.

Give it a shot.

Go find other teachers, then listen to other people and yourself. Listen into the depths and wait for the divine.

Once you quiet your interior life, the signal of your true self becomes easier to discern.
